<header class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="hero-badge"><span class="dot"></span>CASE STUDY — AUTO-RESEARCH</div>
  <h1>Teaching the machine to find its own <em>pipeline bubbles</em></h1>
  <p class="hero-subtitle">How our auto-research loop benchmarks every unit op inside an opaque
  Pallas TPU kernel, reconstructs its DMA / MXU / VPU pipeline as a calibrated timeline, and
  drove a fused attention kernel from 545&thinsp;µs to 420&thinsp;µs — without a profiler ever
  seeing inside it.</p>
  <div class="meta"><span>TPU v6e</span><span>Ragged Paged Attention</span><span>12 min read</span></div>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="stat-number">545<b>→420</b></div><div class="stat-label">kernel latency, µs</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="stat-number"><b>−23%</b></div><div class="stat-label">prefill kernel</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="stat-number"><b>−325</b>µs</div><div class="stat-label">decode pipeline, same method</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="stat-number">21<b>→0.4</b></div><div class="stat-label">empty-kernel entry, µs</div></div>
  </div>
</div></header>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">01 — The Problem</div>
  <h2>A fusion that made everything slower</h2>
  <p>Our ragged paged attention kernel serves mixed prefill/decode batches for LLM inference on
  TPU. The experiment: fuse the <strong>QKV projection, RMS-norm, and RoPE</strong> — normally
  separate XLA ops between transformer layers — directly into the attention kernel. On paper,
  fewer HBM round-trips for Q/K/V and less per-layer overhead. In practice, the first working
  version measured <span class="mono">545µs</span> against a <span class="mono">253µs</span>
  attention-only baseline — adding 292&thinsp;µs to absorb work XLA did in ~200&thinsp;µs.</p>
  <p>Somewhere inside the kernel, time was leaking. But a Pallas kernel is opaque: the profiler
  shows one 545&thinsp;µs custom-call block and nothing inside it.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">02 — Benchmark Every Unit Op</div>
  <h2>If you can't see inside, rebuild it from parts</h2>
  <p>The auto-research harness measures each primitive the kernel is made of, at the kernel's
  <strong>exact tile shapes</strong> — GEMM efficiency at <code>M=256</code> is not GEMM
  efficiency at <code>M=64</code>:</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th>Unit op</th><th>Resource</th><th>Measured</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Q projection GEMM <span class="mono">[256,2560]@[2560,4096]</span></td><td>MXU</td><td class="n">18.65 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>KV projection GEMM (per tile)</td><td>MXU</td><td class="n">3.5 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Attention QK / PV matmul (per 512-token chunk)</td><td>MXU</td><td class="n">5.79 / 6.06 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Q norm + RoPE / K norm + RoPE</td><td>VPU</td><td class="n">6.35 / 3.3 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Softmax accumulate (hides under PV matmul)</td><td>VPU</td><td class="n">1.14 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>x tile fetch / KV-cache tile read or write</td><td>DMA</td><td class="n">1.4 / 2.7 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fused weight matrix, 31 MB HBM→VMEM</td><td>DMA</td><td class="n">19.6 µs</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p>These constants are the vocabulary. Summed, they come to far less than 545&thinsp;µs —
  and that gap between <em>sum of parts</em> and <em>measured whole</em> is the whole game:
  it is all stalls.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">03 — Reconstruct the Pipeline</div>
  <h2>The diagram that names every bubble</h2>
  <p>A TPU core has three engines that can run concurrently: the <strong>MXU</strong> (matrix),
  the <strong>VPU</strong> (vector), and the <strong>DMA engines</strong>. A kernel is fast
  exactly when all three are busy at once. Our tooling replays the kernel's program order —
  every GEMM, norm, and DMA with its true dependencies — through a small event scheduler built
  on the unit-op constants, and renders it as an interactive timeline: one swimlane per engine,
  one hoverable block per op.</p>
  <div class="pull"><p>The discipline that makes it trustworthy: the model's total must match the
  measured total. When it does, every gap in the swimlanes is a real bubble with a name.</p></div>

  <div class="viz">
    <div class="viz-title"><b>BEFORE</b> — first fused version, 545 µs (schematic)</div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">DMA</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:4%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:12%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:5%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:20%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:4%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:23%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:5%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:19%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:4%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">MXU</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:4%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:13%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:7%"></span><span class="seg mxu" style="width:11%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:6%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:9%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:8%"></span><span class="seg mxu" style="width:14%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:7%"></span><span class="seg mxu" style="width:12%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">VPU</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:17%"></span><span class="seg vpu" style="width:7%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:15%"></span><span class="seg vpu2" style="width:6%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:22%"></span><span class="seg vpu" style="width:7%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:14%"></span><span class="seg vpu2" style="width:5%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="viz-note">MXU idles while the VPU norms each projection; K/V computed in a
    separate pass behind a second x fetch; attention blocked behind both.
    <i>Gaps = engine idle.</i></div>
  </div>

  <div class="viz">
    <div class="viz-title"><b>AFTER</b> — pipelined, 420 µs (schematic)</div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">DMA</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:15%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:3%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:6%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:5%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:5%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:6%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:6%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:5%"></span>
      <span class="seg dma" style="width:5%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">MXU</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:2%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:14%"></span>
      <span class="seg mxu" style="width:15%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:5%"></span>
      <span class="seg mxu" style="width:17%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:14%"></span>
      <span class="seg mxu" style="width:18%"></span><span class="seg mxu2" style="width:13%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="lane"><div class="lane-name">VPU</div><div class="bar">
      <span class="seg vpu2" style="width:3%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:12%"></span>
      <span class="seg vpu" style="width:9%"></span><span class="seg vpu2" style="width:8%"></span>
      <span class="seg gap" style="width:5%"></span><span class="seg vpu" style="width:10%"></span>
      <span class="seg vpu2" style="width:9%"></span><span class="seg gap" style="width:6%"></span>
      <span class="seg vpu" style="width:9%"></span></div></div>
    <div class="viz-note">Projection GEMM for tile <i>t+1</i> issued before tile <i>t</i>'s
    attention; norms run in the shadow of matmuls; the 31 MB weight copy streams under the
    prologue fetches. <i>MXU duty cycle is the ceiling now.</i></div>
  </div>

  <p>Three bubbles were obvious the moment the first calibrated diagram rendered: the MXU lane
  had a gap after <em>every</em> Q projection (waiting on the VPU norm), K/V for each
  1024-token block stalled attention behind its own DMA + GEMM + norm chain, and the same
  x&nbsp;tile was fetched twice — once for Q, once for K/V. None of this is visible in a
  profiler trace. All of it is obvious in the picture.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">04 — Fix · Measure · Recalibrate</div>
  <h2>Every fix earns its place with a number</h2>
  <p>Each iteration follows the same loop: roofline-check the idea (if the math says it can't
  win, don't prototype it), patch the kernel, benchmark, re-fit the diagram. The wins, in the
  order they landed:</p>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">01</div>
      <h3>Overlap the Q projection with attention<span class="delta">−28 µs</span></h3>
      <p>Issue tile <em>t+1</em>'s projection GEMM before tile <em>t</em>'s attention loops. The MXU
      runs them back-to-back while the VPU norms tile <em>t+1</em> under the attention matmuls.
      The MXU lane closed up; the VPU lane went from bursty to continuously occupied.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">02</div>
      <h3>Compute K/V per tile from already-resident x<span class="delta">−1 DMA stream, −1 GEMM stall</span></h3>
      <p>Each 256-row x tile — already in VMEM for the Q projection — produces its K/V rows,
      written eagerly to the KV cache; later tiles re-read them as ordinary cached tokens.
      One x load instead of two, and the 3.5 µs KV GEMM tucks between attention loops instead
      of damming them.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">03</div>
      <h3>Fuse the weights once, at load time<span class="delta">−1.4 ms / model step</span></h3>
      <p>Concatenating [W<sub>q</sub>&thinsp;|&thinsp;W<sub>k</sub>&thinsp;|&thinsp;W<sub>v</sub>]
      inside the jitted step made XLA re-materialize ~30 MB per layer, every step. Building it
      once after checkpoint load turned the weights into a pass-through operand — found by the
      diagram's XLA-side sibling: dumping the compiled HLO and reading what surrounds the kernel.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">04</div>
      <h3>Stream the weights under the prologue<span class="delta">21 → 0.4 µs empty-kernel entry</span></h3>
      <p>Declaring the fused weights as a VMEM operand made Pallas block-copy 31 MB on
      <em>every kernel entry — including empty ones</em>. Roofline arithmetic caught it:
      an "empty" kernel measured 21 µs ≈ 31.4 MB ÷ 1.6 TB/s. Now an async DMA issued as the
      prologue's first instruction overlaps the x and KV fetches, with a one-shot semaphore
      wait before first use.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">05</div>
      <h3>Ablate, don't assume<span class="delta">the −65 µs that wasn't</span></h3>
      <p>The harness patches any region out of the kernel, measures, and auto-reverts. It
      attributed the remaining cost precisely (Q GEMM 67 µs, KV GEMM 29 µs, RoPE ~20 µs — all
      near roofline) and produced our favorite negative result: <em>removing</em> the Q norm made
      the kernel 65 µs <em>slower</em>. Its store pattern was load-bearing for the pipeline.
      Intuition would never guess that; the harness measured it in two minutes.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">05 — Results</div>
  <h2>Faster than XLA at XLA's own job</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Baseline</th><th>First fused</th><th>After auto-research</th></tr>
    <tr><td>RPA prefill kernel (2048 tok, v6e-1)</td><td class="n">253 µs</td><td class="n">545 µs</td><td class="n"><span class="win">~420 µs</span></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Work absorbed into the kernel</td><td>—</td><td>proj + norm + RoPE</td><td>proj + norm + RoPE</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Same work done externally in XLA</td><td class="n">~200 µs</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p>The fused kernel now performs the projection + norm + RoPE work <strong>faster than XLA
  does it standalone</strong> (~170 µs marginal vs ~200 µs) while also eliminating the Q/K/V
  HBM round-trips and per-layer op overhead. Pointed at the decode kernel, the same method
  found a different class of bug — a DMA prefetch serialized behind a cache write it didn't
  depend on — and recovered <span class="mono">325µs</span> there. The diagram's DMA lane
  showed reads queuing behind writes; the fix was three conditions.</p>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-label">06 — Takeaways</div>
  <h2>What generalizes</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">01</div><h3>A calibrated model beats a profiler for opaque kernels</h3>
      <p>When the model total matches the measured total, every swimlane gap is a real bubble with a name.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">02</div><h3>Benchmark unit ops at exact shapes</h3>
      <p>The constants must come from the kernel's actual tile sizes, or the model lies to you politely.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">03</div><h3>Gate ideas with a roofline before prototyping</h3>
      <p>Half our ideas died for the price of a division. The other half arrived pre-quantified.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="card-number">04</div><h3>Watch the edges of the kernel</h3>
      <p>Two of the biggest wins were about how operands cross the XLA↔Pallas boundary, not the kernel body at all.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>The whole loop — unit benchmarks, ablation harness, roofline checker, trace differ, and
